    "['sysname [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [0, 0], 'trans_command': 'hostname [parameter1]', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'hostname [parameter1]'}]]": 1,
    "['radius-server group [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'aaa group server radius rd5', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'aaa group server radius rd5', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\naaa group server radius rd5\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Huawei, `radius-server group rd5` creates a RADIUS server group named \"rd5\".\\n- The equivalent Cisco command is `aaa group server radius rd5`, which similarly creates a RADIUS server group with the specified name.\\n- The subsequent server configurations (IP, port, key) would be configured separately in Cisco under this group.', 'trans_template': 'aaa group server radius rd5'}]]": 1,
    "['radius-server authentication [parameter1] [parameter2] weight [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'radius-server host 10.6.55.55 auth-port 1645 key 051B1C224D0A324E1D', 'parent_command': ['aaa group server radius rd5'], 'root': 'aaa group server radius rd5', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nradius-server host 10.6.55.55 auth-port 1645 key 051B1C224D0A324E1D\\n##\\n\\nNote: The Cisco equivalent combines the authentication server configuration with the shared key in a single command. The \"weight 0\" parameter from Huawei doesn\\'t have a direct equivalent in Cisco, as Cisco doesn\\'t use weight-based RADIUS server selection in the same way. The key is assumed to be the same as in the Huawei configuration\\'s radius-server shared-key-cipher parameter.', 'trans_template': 'radius-server host 10.6.55.55 auth-port 1645 key 051B1C224D0A324E1D'}]]": 1,
    "['radius-server accounting [parameter1] [parameter2] weight [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'radius-server host 10.6.55.55 auth-port 1645 acct-port 1646 key 051B1C224D0A324E1D', 'parent_command': ['aaa group server radius rd5'], 'root': 'aaa group server radius rd5', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nradius-server host 10.6.55.55 auth-port 1645 acct-port 1646 key 051B1C224D0A324E1D\\n##\\n\\nNote: While this is the closest Cisco equivalent, there are some differences to be aware of:\\n1. Cisco combines both authentication and accounting server definitions in one command\\n2. The \"weight\" parameter isn\\'t directly equivalent in Cisco - server priority is handled differently\\n3. The shared key is specified in the main command rather than in a separate configuration\\n4. Cisco uses \"auth-port\" and \"acct-port\" parameters to specify the different ports', 'trans_template': 'radius-server host 10.6.55.55 auth-port 1645 acct-port 1646 key 051B1C224D0A324E1D'}]]": 1,
    "['radius-server shared-key-cipher [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'radius-server host 10.6.55.55 auth-port 1645 acct-port 1646 key 051B1C224D0A324E1D', 'parent_command': ['aaa group server radius rd5'], 'root': 'aaa group server radius rd5', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nradius-server host 10.6.55.55 auth-port 1645 acct-port 1646 key 051B1C224D0A324E1D\\n##\\n\\nNote: In Cisco:\\n1. The shared key is configured directly under the `radius-server host` command\\n2. The authentication and accounting ports are specified in the same command\\n3. The key is shown in plaintext (Cisco doesn\\'t typically mark keys as cipher-text in config)\\n4. There\\'s no separate \"group\" concept like Huawei - the parameters are applied directly to the RADIUS server definition', 'trans_template': 'radius-server host 10.6.55.55 auth-port 1645 acct-port 1646 key 051B1C224D0A324E1D'}]]": 1,
